Wait — one subtlety with null-marking CORO_LIVE: `close_live_gens` calls close_py on each non-null. But a coroutine could have been closed via `Coro::close()` — which calls live_remove (nulls its slot). Fine. Could a coroutine be double-registered? No.

But — the earlier code had a `still` check because close_py of one gen could CLOSE others (running finally blocks that close other generators)? close_py runs user code (finally blocks) which could close another generator → its slot nulled → but we already snapshot the list; calling close_py on an already-closed (nulled later) gen: close_py checks `if !self.is_suspended() { self.close(); return Ok(None) }` — close() on already-closed: `result = Some(Ok(None)); free_stack(); live_remove` — free_stack on null stack is no-op. So safe to call on already-closed gens. The old code's `still` check protected against... reading freed memory if the gen was dropped entirely! If user code during shutdown drops the last reference to a generator, Drop runs → frees the Coro → close_live_gens then dereferences a freed pointer! The old code re-checked `contains(&c)` — but the stale slot... in the OLD code, live_remove REMOVED from the vec, so contains would be false → skip. In my new code, the slot stays (nulled) — but if the Coro was DROPPED (freed), the slot is null only if Drop ran live_remove — Drop does call live_remove (nulls slot). So a dropped gen → null slot → skipped. Good.

But what if close_py during iteration causes another gen's Drop → nulls its slot — we already snapshotted its pointer → we'd call close_py on a FREED pointer! The old `still` check re-verified liveness (contains) — non-null after removal... old code: dropped gen removed from list → contains false → skip. New code: null slot — but my snapshot doesn't re-check! I must re-check the slot before dereferencing:

```rust
for c in live {
    let still = CORO_LIVE.with(|l| l.borrow().get(unsafe { (*c).live_idx }) == Some(&c));
    ...
}
```
Reading `(*c).live_idx` — c might be freed if dropped... deref of freed pointer — UB! Order: c was non-null at snapshot; it can only become nulled via live_remove (which runs in Drop or finish). If dropped, the memory freed — reading live_idx is use-after-free.

Safer: snapshot (ptr, idx) pairs; re-check slot[idx] == ptr BEFORE dereferencing:
```rust
let live: Vec<(*mut Coro, usize)> = CORO_LIVE.with(|l| l.borrow().iter().enumerate().filter(|(_, c)| !c.is_null()).map(|(i, c)| (*c, i)).collect());
for (c, i) in live {
    let still = CORO_LIVE.with(|l| l.borrow().get(i) == Some(&c));
    if still {
        unsafe { if let Err(E(e)) = (*c).close_py() { gen_unraisable(&e); } }
    }
}
```
Reading slot[i] is safe (Vec memory managed); compare without dereferencing c. If c was dropped and slot nulled → skip. If slot somehow reused... indices never reused (only append + null). Good.

Let me fix close_live_gens accordingly.

